

✅ Does JMeter Check Status Code by Default?
No, JMeter does not automatically validate that the response is HTTP 200 unless you add an assertion.
So why does it show as ✅ green (pass) in "View Results Tree"?
Because:
JMeter considers any successful connection (e.g., server responded) as a pass.
It doesn't care about response code or page content unless you explicitly tell it to.
🟢 Green = No sampler or connection-level error
🔴 Red = Connection failed (e.g., timeout, DNS failure, etc.)
💡 A 500 Internal Server Error will still show as green unless you add an assertion that fails when the response code ≠ 200.
🧪 What Is an Assertion in JMeter?
An Assertion checks if your response is correct and expected.
You can assert:
✅ Status code (e.g., 200 OK)
🧾 Response contains specific text (e.g., “Welcome”)
📄 JSON/XML structure
📏 Response size/time
🔧 How to Add Assertions
🔹 Add Response Code Assertion (e.g., 200 OK)
Right-click on a request
→ Add → Assertions → Response Assertion
Under Response Field to Test, choose:
Response Code
Under Pattern Matching Rules, choose:
Equals
In Patterns to Test, type:
200
✅ This will fail if response ≠ 200.
🔹 Add Text Assertion (e.g., Page contains “Welcome”)
Add another Response Assertion
Under Response Field to Test, select:
Text Response
In Patterns to Test, enter:
e.g., Welcome or Dashboard or a unique string
✅ This will ensure the page loaded expected content.
🧠 Why Assertions Matter
Without Assertions	With Assertions
Only checks if the server responded	Checks if the response is correct
May miss failures like HTTP 500 or broken pages	Catches logic errors, bad sessions, etc.
✅ False sense of success	✅ True application validation
🔍 Example Setup
You're testing login flow. You might:
Assert 200 on login POST
Assert "Welcome, John" on dashboard page
Assert "Logout" button is present
That gives confidence that the session worked — not just that the server responded.
